<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  office.world — a small, fully OFFLINE multi-room office floor for the
  Husky VSLAM sim. Same offline-only approach as before (shapes + Gazebo's
  built-in materials, no fuel.gazebosim.org downloads).

  Floor plan (top-down, +X east, +Y north), 14 x 12 m overall:

      Y=6  +================+================+
           |   NW Office    |  NE Meeting Rm |
      Y=2  +======+                  +=======+
           |                                 |
           |         corridor (3.8 m)        |
           |                                 |
      Y=-2 +======+                  +=======+
           |   SW Office    |  SE Break Room |
      Y=-6 +================+================+
          X=-7             X=0              X=7

  The robot spawns at the origin, in the corridor. Each room opens onto the
  corridor through a 2.4 m doorway; rooms are not directly connected to each
  other, only via the corridor - so driving between rooms and back is what
  gives RTAB-Map real loop-closure opportunities instead of one long straight
  pass.

  ===========================================================================
  WHY EVERY SURFACE HERE IS TEXTURED - and why that is the whole point
  ===========================================================================

  A stereo camera does not measure distance the way a LiDAR does. It finds
  small, recognisable PATTERNS in the left and right images, matches them, and
  works out depth and motion from how they shift. Those patterns are called
  "features". No features means no matching, which means no odometry at all -
  the camera literally cannot tell whether it moved.

  An earlier version of this file gave every wall a DIFFERENT material to stop
  RTAB-Map confusing one room for another. That fixed the confusion, but it
  used flat paint colours (pure red, pure cyan, pure yellow...). A flat colour
  has no pattern anywhere on it: every pixel looks like its neighbours. When
  we measured the running simulation, the feature detector found just
  13 usable points in a whole 1920x1200 image - and RTAB-Map needs at least
  20 to compute a single pose. Odometry failed on every frame, and the map
  came out bent and wavy.

  So every surface below now uses a material with a real image texture -
  brickwork, wood grain, paving, tiling - and every one of the 12 wall
  segments still uses a DIFFERENT one. That satisfies both requirements at
  once:

    * TEXTURED  -> plenty of features, so odometry can track motion
    * DISTINCT  -> each place looks unique, so loop closure does not weld two
                   different rooms together

  Two practical notes on texture SCALE, which matters as much as presence:

    * Gazebo paints one copy of a texture across each face of a box. A single
      14 m wall therefore shows one very stretched image, whereas a 0.5 m crate
      shows the full image at full detail. That is why the floor below is built
      from a grid of tiles rather than one big slab, and why the small props
      matter more than their size suggests - they are the richest, sharpest
      feature sources in the scene.
    * The props also give each room its own look, which is what loop-closure
      recognition actually keys on.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <world name="office">
    <include><uri>model://sun</uri></include>
    <include><uri>model://ground_plane</uri></include>

    <!-- ==== textured floor ==============================================
         VISUAL ONLY - there is deliberately no <collision> here. The physics
         floor is still the ground_plane included above, exactly as before, so
         the robot's contact and friction behaviour is unchanged. This is a
         thin 4 mm skin laid 2 mm over it purely so the camera has something
         to look at pointing down the corridor.

         Split into a 4 x 4 grid because one 13.8 m slab would stretch a single
         copy of the wood texture across the whole room and wash out the detail;
         each ~3.4 x 3.0 m tile instead carries a full, sharp copy.
    -->
    <model name="floor_skin">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="tiles">
        <visual name="f00"><pose>-5.175 -4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodFloor</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f01"><pose>-1.725 -4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Pedestrian</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f02"><pose>1.725 -4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Road</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f03"><pose>5.175 -4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/CeilingTiled</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f10"><pose>-5.175 -1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Road</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f11"><pose>-1.725 -1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/CeilingTiled</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f12"><pose>1.725 -1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodFloor</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f13"><pose>5.175 -1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Pedestrian</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f20"><pose>-5.175 1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Pedestrian</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f21"><pose>-1.725 1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodFloor</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f22"><pose>1.725 1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/CeilingTiled</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f23"><pose>5.175 1.475 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Road</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f30"><pose>-5.175 4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/CeilingTiled</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f31"><pose>-1.725 4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Road</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f32"><pose>1.725 4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Pedestrian</name></script></material></visual>
        <visual name="f33"><pose>5.175 4.425 0.002 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>3.45 2.95 0.004</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodFloor</name></script></material></visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ==== building shell + interior walls (one static model) ====
         All 12 segments textured, and all 12 DIFFERENT - see header. -->
    <model name="building">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="walls">

        <!-- outer shell - all four sides distinct -->
        <collision name="outer_n_c"><pose>0 6 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>14 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="outer_n_v"><pose>0 6 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>14 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Bricks</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="outer_s_c"><pose>0 -6 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>14 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="outer_s_v"><pose>0 -6 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>14 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/PaintedWall</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="outer_e_c"><pose>7 0 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 12 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="outer_e_v"><pose>7 0 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 12 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/CeilingTiled</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="outer_w_c"><pose>-7 0 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 12 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="outer_w_v"><pose>-7 0 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 12 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Residential</name></script></material></visual>

        <!-- corridor / north-rooms wall (y=2), split around the two doorways -->
        <collision name="cn_1_c"><pose>-5.85 2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="cn_1_v"><pose>-5.85 2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Wood</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="cn_2_c"><pose>0 2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>4.6 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="cn_2_v"><pose>0 2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>4.6 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/BuildingFrame</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="cn_3_c"><pose>5.85 2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="cn_3_v"><pose>5.85 2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Steps</name></script></material></visual>

        <!-- corridor / south-rooms wall (y=-2), split around the two doorways -->
        <collision name="cs_1_c"><pose>-5.85 -2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="cs_1_v"><pose>-5.85 -2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodPallet</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="cs_2_c"><pose>0 -2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>4.6 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="cs_2_v"><pose>0 -2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>4.6 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Motorway</name></script></material></visual>
        <collision name="cs_3_c"><pose>5.85 -2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="cs_3_v"><pose>5.85 -2 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>2.3 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Trunk</name></script></material></visual>

        <!-- NW/NE dividing wall -->
        <collision name="div_n_c"><pose>0 4 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 4 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="div_n_v"><pose>0 4 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 4 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Primary</name></script></material></visual>

        <!-- SW/SE dividing wall -->
        <collision name="div_s_c"><pose>0 -4 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 4 2.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual   name="div_s_v"><pose>0 -4 1.25 0 0 0</pose><geometry><box><size>0.2 4 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Secondary</name></script></material></visual>

      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ================= LAYOUT REVISION 2026-08-23 ====================
         The prop layout below was edited by hand in the Gazebo GUI during
         the first office-room-1 exploration session and saved via
         File > Save World As; those changes were merged back into this
         file. Two kinds of change were made:

           DELETED (17 props): nw_chair, nw_bookshelf, ne_chair_1,
           ne_chair_2, sw_chair, se_counter, se_stool_1, se_stool_2,
           cor_crate_e, cor_pillar, nw_crate, ne_crate, ne_column,
           ne_low_box, sw_crate, se_crate, se_column.
           The intent was to clear furniture blocking the doorways: the
           three props that routes/office1_full.yaml names as making rooms
           unreachable (ne_column, nw_crate, sw_crate) are among them.

           RELOCATED (8 props): reception_desk, nw_desk, ne_table, sw_desk,
           se_table, cor_crate_w, cor_box_e, nw_barrel.

         CONSEQUENCE FOR DRIVING. Seven of the eight relocations moved a
         prop TOWARD the corridor. Three now overlap the corridor spine at
         y ~ 0 by more than the robot's 0.598 m body radius
         (reception_desk, se_table, cor_crate_w), and cor_box_e grazes it.
         routes/office1_full.yaml, written against the ORIGINAL layout,
         cannot be driven on this world: 27 of its 63 primitives collide.
         Regenerate it from a hand-driven recording
         (sidewalk_sim/scripts/route_from_drive.py) or retire it.

         CONSEQUENCE FOR MAPPING. This removes 63 % of the small feature
         props that the feature-props header below identifies as the
         scene's feature backbone. Run the per-room ORB feature-count gate
         (docs/OPERATIONS.md) before trusting any mapping result from this
         layout. A flat-colour version of this world once yielded 13
         keypoints against RTAB-Map's minimum of 20.
    -->

    <!-- ==== corridor landmark (reception desk, mid-corridor) ==== -->
    <model name="reception_desk"><static>true</static><pose>-3.53785 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>1.5 0.5 1.0</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>1.5 0.5 1.0</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Wood</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- ==== NW Office: desk ==== -->
    <model name="nw_desk"><static>true</static><pose>-4.71039 4.45801 0.375 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>1.2 0.6 0.75</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>1.2 0.6 0.75</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodPallet</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- ==== NE Meeting Room: table ==== -->
    <model name="ne_table"><static>true</static><pose>3.5 4.38204 0.375 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>2.4 1.0 0.75</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>2.4 1.0 0.75</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Wood</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- ==== SW Office: desk, cabinet ==== -->
    <model name="sw_desk"><static>true</static><pose>-3.39663 -3.94467 0.375 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>1.2 0.6 0.75</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>1.2 0.6 0.75</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Road</name></script></material></visual></link></model>
    <model name="sw_cabinet"><static>true</static><pose>-1 -5.3 0.6 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>0.6 0.4 1.2</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>0.6 0.4 1.2</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Pedestrian</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- ==== SE Break Room: round table ==== -->
    <model name="se_table"><static>true</static><pose>3.50432 -4 0.375 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.5</radius><length>0.75</length></cylinder></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.5</radius><length>0.75</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Runway</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- ================= feature props =================================
         Small textured objects, 0.3-1.0 m. These are the sharpest feature
         sources in the world: a box this size shows one FULL copy of its
         texture per face, where a 14 m wall stretches a single copy across
         its whole length.

         LAYOUT REVISED 2026-08-23; see the LAYOUT REVISION note further
         up this file. The original placement rule (every prop clear of the
         corridor centreline and clear of the four doorways, with a distinct
         per-room combination so rooms do not look alike to loop-closure)
         NO LONGER HOLDS:
           * cor_crate_w (1.44, -0.03) and cor_box_e (4.14, -0.04) now sit
             ON the corridor centreline, in the robot's main driving lane.
           * Only four of the original nine feature props remain
             (cor_crate_w, cor_box_e, nw_barrel, sw_barrel). The NE meeting
             room and SE break room keep no feature prop at all, only
             their furniture (ne_table, se_table).
         Re-derive any scripted route against THIS layout, and re-check the
         per-room ORB feature count before trusting a mapping result.
    -->

    <!-- corridor -->
    <model name="cor_crate_w"><static>true</static><pose>1.43781 -0.031077 0.25 0 0 0.01149</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>0.5 0.5 0.5</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>0.5 0.5 0.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/WoodPallet</name></script></material></visual></link></model>
    <model name="cor_box_e"><static>true</static><pose>4.13895 -0.035371 0.35 0 0 0.008045</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><box><size>0.7 0.5 0.7</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><box><size>0.7 0.5 0.7</size></box></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Runway</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- NW Office -->
    <model name="nw_barrel"><static>true</static><pose>-1.70856 4.34149 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.3</radius><length>0.9</length></cylinder></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.3</radius><length>0.9</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Trunk</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- SW Office -->
    <model name="sw_barrel"><static>true</static><pose>-6.2 -5.0 0.4 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="l"><collision name="c"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.28</radius><length>0.8</length></cylinder></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="v"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.28</radius><length>0.8</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><script><uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri><name>Gazebo/Grass</name></script></material></visual></link></model>

    <!-- ==== overhead presentation camera ===============================
         WHAT THIS IS. One downward-looking colour camera, attached to
         nothing, hanging 18 m over the centre of the building and publishing
         a plain image on /overhead_cam/image_raw. Point a recorder at that
         topic and every mapping run of this world produces a top-down video
         of the robot driving the floor plan, at no extra effort during the
         run. There is no ceiling in this world - only walls and a floor skin
         - so a camera above it looks straight down into every room.

         WHY IT LIVES INSIDE THE WORLD INSTEAD OF BEING A SCREEN RECORDER.
         Measured runs are launched with the graphical client switched off
         (gui:=false) and no windows on screen, which is most of why they run
         roughly four times faster than a watched run. There is consequently
         nothing on screen for a screen recorder to capture. The simulation
         server, however, renders camera sensors inside its own process
         whether or not any window exists - that is the confirmed rule in
         ENGINEERING_NOTES.md section 5 - so a camera placed in the world yields a real
         rendered picture at the full headless speed. The top-down picture at
         logs/sidewalk_evaluation/results/phase1/fix_verify/gazebo_birdseye.png
         was produced exactly this way with a temporary camera that was never
         kept. This is that same technique, made permanent.

         THE CAVEAT THAT WILL CATCH SOMEBODY OUT. Rendering any camera at all
         needs a graphics context, and the simulation server takes that from
         the DISPLAY and XAUTHORITY environment variables. Launch over a
         remote shell without both of them exported and this camera is never
         created: no images, no camera information, and no error that names
         the cause - just the line "Unable to create CameraSensor. Rendering
         is disabled." in the server log, after which everything else carries
         on normally. The robot's own stereo camera has exactly the same
         requirement, so in practice a run that produced no overhead video is
         a run that produced no camera images at all, and the fix is the same
         one: export both variables before launching.

         HOW THE 18 m HEIGHT WAS COMPUTED. It was derived, not guessed, and
         it is deliberately NOT the same as office2.world's - that building
         is larger and its camera sits higher.

         The lens is set to a 1.047 radian (60.0 degree) horizontal field of
         view. The simulator derives the vertical field of view from the
         image shape, so an 800 by 600 picture covers a patch of floor with
         the same 4 : 3 proportions as the picture itself:

             half-width  a = height * tan(field of view / 2) = height * 0.5772
             half-height b = a * 600 / 800                   = a * 0.75

         The building is 14 m east-west by 12 m north-south, so its floor
         half-extents are 7 m and 6 m. That is not the whole requirement,
         because the walls are 2.5 m tall and this is a perspective lens, not
         an overhead orthographic drawing. Seen from a point directly above
         the centre, the TOP outer edge of a wall standing at horizontal
         distance d appears to lie at

             d * height / (height - 2.5)

         so the frame has to cover more than the floor footprint or the tops
         of the outer walls are sliced off at the edges. Requiring a further
         10 % margin beyond even that:

             east-west   0.5772 * (height - 2.5) >= 7 * 1.10 -> height >= 15.84 m
             north-south 0.4329 * (height - 2.5) >= 6 * 1.10 -> height >= 17.75 m

         North-south is the binding constraint, because this building is
         squarer (14 : 12) than the picture is (4 : 3). Rounded up to a flat
         18 m, which delivers:

             ground coverage        20.78 m east-west by 15.59 m north-south
             margin past wall tops  27.8 % east-west, 11.8 % north-south
             clear border at floor  3.39 m beyond the east and west walls,
                                    1.79 m beyond the north and south walls
             scale                  26 mm of floor per pixel, so the Husky
                                    (about 1.0 by 0.67 m) is roughly 38 by 26
                                    pixels - small, but unmistakably a robot
                                    moving between rooms, which is all this
                                    clip has to show.

         WHICH WAY IS UP IN THE PICTURE. A camera here looks along its own
         forward axis with its own upward axis at the top of the frame.
         Pitching it a quarter turn (1.5708 radians) nose-down aims it at the
         floor; the further quarter turn of yaw then rolls the frame so that
         north (+Y) is at the top and east (+X) is at the right. The video
         therefore has the same orientation as the floor-plan sketch at the
         top of this file, so the two can be held side by side while
         explaining a run.

         WHY THIS CANNOT DISTURB A MAPPING RUN.
           * It is not attached to the robot. It is its own static model at a
             fixed pose, contributing no transform, no joint and no mass to
             the robot.
           * Its link carries no collision shape and no visual shape at all,
             only the sensor. The robot cannot drive into it, the navigation
             costmap never sees it, and the robot's own stereo camera cannot
             see it even looking straight up.
           * It publishes on /overhead_cam/image_raw and
             /overhead_cam/camera_info, and nothing in the mapping stack
             subscribes to either. sidewalk_slam/launch/rtabmap_common.launch
             was read to confirm this: it subscribes only to the colour,
             depth, left, right, camera-information, inertial and odometry
             topics beneath the robot camera's own namespace (zedx_front /
             zed_node by default), plus its own internal bundled image topic.
           * It costs roughly 7 % of what the robot's stereo pair already
             costs: 800 by 600 at 10 frames per simulated second is 4.8
             million pixels per second and 14.4 megabytes per second of image
             traffic, against 69 million pixels per second and 207 megabytes
             per second for two 1920 by 1200 colour lenses at 15 hertz. That
             ratio is the point - this machine is already carrying physics,
             the stereo camera and the mapping software at once, and
             over-subscribing it has caused real failures before (see
             docs/OPERATIONS.md on the memory and queue-depth incident).

         WHY 800 BY 600 AT 10 FRAMES PER SECOND. This picture is watched, not
         measured: no pose is ever computed from it, so the only requirement
         is that a person can see where the robot is. 10 frames of SIMULATED
         time per second, played back at 10 frames per second, shows the
         motion at true speed no matter what multiple of real time the
         simulation happened to be running at. Raising either number buys
         nothing and takes graphics time away from the stereo camera, which
         is the one that has to keep up.
    -->
    <model name="overhead_cam">
      <static>true</static>
      <!-- 18 m above the building centre. Pitch 1.5708 aims the lens straight
           down; the matching yaw puts north at the top of the frame. -->
      <pose>0 0 18.0 0 1.5708 1.5708</pose>
      <link name="lens">
        <sensor name="overhead_camera" type="camera">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>10</update_rate>
          <!-- No frustum drawn in the graphical client: it would sit across
               the middle of the scene during hand-driving sessions. -->
          <visualize>false</visualize>
          <camera name="overhead">
            <horizontal_fov>1.047</horizontal_fov>
            <image><width>800</width><height>600</height><format>R8G8B8</format></image>
            <!-- Clipping is measured along the lens axis, and the floor is
                 exactly 18 m along it, so the far plane only has to clear 18.
                 40 m leaves room to raise the camera later without silently
                 clipping the floor away. -->
            <clip><near>1.0</near><far>40.0</far></clip>
            <!-- Deliberately NO <noise> here. The robot's stereo camera
                 carries Gaussian noise on purpose, because its covariance has
                 to be honest (ENGINEERING_NOTES.md section 2.3). This one is only ever
                 looked at, and grain would just make the video harder to
                 read. -->
          </camera>
          <plugin name="overhead_cam_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_camera.so">
            <alwaysOn>true</alwaysOn>
            <!-- 0.0 means "publish whenever the sensor renders", so the
                 10 hertz above is the single place the rate is set. -->
            <updateRate>0.0</updateRate>
            <!-- Camera name plus image topic name is what forms the published
                 topic: /overhead_cam/image_raw, with /overhead_cam/camera_info
                 beside it. -->
            <cameraName>overhead_cam</cameraName>
            <imageTopicName>image_raw</imageTopicName>
            <cameraInfoTopicName>camera_info</cameraInfoTopicName>
            <!-- Nothing publishes a transform for this frame and nothing
                 needs one: the picture is watched in an image viewer or
                 written to a video file, never projected into the map. -->
            <frameName>overhead_cam_optical_frame</frameName>
            <distortionK1>0.0</distortionK1>
            <distortionK2>0.0</distortionK2>
            <distortionK3>0.0</distortionK3>
            <distortionT1>0.0</distortionT1>
            <distortionT2>0.0</distortionT2>
          </plugin>
        </sensor>
      </link>
    </model>

    <physics type="ode"><real_time_update_rate>1000</real_time_update_rate><max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size></physics>
    <scene><ambient>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</ambient><background>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</background><shadows>false</shadows></scene>
  </world>
</sdf>
